AB  - Flooding is one of the most disruptive urban hazards, causing major damage to infrastructure and livelihoods, especially in rapidly growing cities. This study analyzes sewer overflow complaints, rainfall intensity, and inundation points across Hyderabad, India, to improve flood preparedness. A strong spatial match was found between rainfall and inundation priority maps, with 9 of 11 watersheds sharing identical rankings, confirming rainfall data as a reliable flood indicator. Comparison with Overflow Priority Zones derived from citizen complaints on X (formerly Twitter) showed overall agreement across high, medium, and low-risk areas, with discrepancies mainly in underreported zones. To apply these insights, a customized API was developed to link X data with the Hyderabad Metropolitan Water Supply and Sewerage Board (HMWSSB) database and visualize results through an ArcGIS Enterprise dashboard. This integration enables near real-time updates, improving flood risk communication and sewage complaint management through multi-source data fusion.
