AB  - This study addresses the critical challenge of missing data and prediction in hydrology. Hydrological data are essential for hazard forecasting, environmental protection, recreation, and agriculture, yet data gaps and errors are inevitable. Using United States Geological Survey Iowa stream gauge stations as a case study, graph neural network (GNN) models are developed for streamflow data imputation and prediction. These models are compared with statistical, machine learning (ML), and deep learning (DL) approaches. Specifically, a Time Series Graph Neural Network (HydroTGNN), adapted from traffic data applications, is proposed to integrate temporal and spatial dependencies through specialized convolutional modules. The study further examines the influence of temporal variations, upstream–downstream directionality, and gauge station locations on imputation accuracy. Results demonstrate that HydroTGNN effectively captures hydrological dynamics via its graph structure and achieves superior performance, confirming that GNN-based modeling provides a powerful framework for representing the spatiotemporal behavior of streamflow systems.
