AB  - This study proposes a coupled model for monthly runoff simulation by integrating a process-based hydrological model with a data-driven deep learning model for multi-site runoff simulation. Specifically, the process-based model is used to simulate watershed hydrological processes and extract physical characteristic parameters of each sub-basin as model inputs. A tree-structured long short-term memory (LSTM) model is then introduced to capture the spatio-temporal relationships across sub-basins. The decision tree combined with Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP) is employed to analyze the impact of each physical factor on model performance. The results indicate that precipitation and evapotranspiration play dominant roles in reducing model error. Although surface runoff, groundwater flow, and lateral flow have relatively smaller contributions to error reduction, the inclusion of these physical variables still enhances model performance, as evidenced by improvements in the KGE. In this optimal case, the NSEs can reach up to 0.85 at most. Spatial heterogeneity analysis revealed that nearby sub-basins contributed more to runoff generation. However, mid-range areas, possibly affected by human activities, exhibit an abnormally amplified impact of lateral flow on downstream gauge stations. Three alternative processing strategies are compared to demonstrate that incorporating tree-structured model and accounting for the hydrological response characteristics of sub-basins leads to the most accurate simulation results.
