KW  - Open-channel systems
AB  - Accurate modelling of open-channel systems remains challenging due to dynamic hydraulics and coupled boundary–disturbance interactions. Existing linear parameter-varying (LPV) models often rely on a single variable, limiting their ability to characterize coupled interactions, and lacking quantitative applicability criteria to assess model validity. This study develops an independent-scheduling variables LPV integral–delay–zero (ISV–LPV–IDZ) model, in which low-frequency parameters are governed by upstream discharge and downstream water-level, while high-frequency ones depend on disturbance amplitude and spectral center frequency. A boundary identification method informed by high-frequency response energy defines the model's applicability domain. Results demonstrate that ISV–LPV–IDZ model reduces the full-period RMSE by 83% and the steady-state error by 86% compared with LPV–ID model while maintaining boundary identification errors around 1 cm. High-frequency response energy is primarily governed by spectral center frequency and decreases by approximately 70%–95% as steady-state discharge increases from 80 m3/s to 150 m3/s.
